----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, N: c3-a4, N: c3-b1, N: c3-b5, N: c3-d5, N: c3-e2, N: c3-e4, Q: d1-d2, Q: d1-d3, Q: d1-d4, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, K: e1-d2, P: e5-e6, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Several moves are promising, but key considerations include development, control of the center, and potential threats. The knight on c3 has good activity and can influence the center. Advancing pawns like a2-a4 or b2-b3 may help control space, but are less impactful. The pawn on e5 is advanced and can push to e6, potentially threatening material or opening lines. The bishop on f1 can develop toward e2 or d3, supporting central play. The queen on d1 has some diagonal options, but immediate development to d3 or d4 could increase pressure.\n\nNotably, P: e5-e6 stands out because:\n- It gains significant space.\n- It threatens to open the e-file for the rook or queen later.\n- It puts indirect pressure on the black king by advancing into enemy territory.\n- It avoids blocking the e5 pawn with other pieces while maximizing its value.\n\nOther moves like N: c3-d5 or Q: d1-d4 also offer activity, but e5-e6 increases tension and limits Black’s responses, possibly leading to tactical opportunities.\n\nReason: Pushing the e5 pawn to e6 maximizes initiative, gains critical space near the opponent's king, and potentially weakens Black's kingside structure. This move increases pressure and opens future attacking chances, especially if Black captures (exd7), which would open the e-file.\n\nAnswer: P: e5-e6"}
