----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, N: c3-a4, N: c3-b1, N: c3-b5, N: c3-d5, N: c3-e2, N: c3-e4, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nLooking at the current position, White has a relatively undeveloped position with limited active pieces. The knight on c3 is already developed and has several options, while the bishops on c1 and f1 are still behind pawns. Black, on the other hand, has some active pieces — two knights on e6 and f6, a dark-squared bishop on b7, and a queen on f5 — which could pose threats in the near term. Notably, the black king is still in the center (e8), and White might have chances to open lines quickly.\n\nKey considerations:\n- **Pawn breaks**: Pushing pawns like a4 or g4 could gain space, but may also weaken the structure.\n- **Development**: Moving the bishops (especially Bc1) helps activate pieces. Bc1-e3 or Bc1-f4 targets key central squares and eyes the weak f7-pawn.\n- **Knight activity**: Nc3 can jump to d5, forking potential if Black plays ...e6 or ...e5, or to e4, applying pressure.\n- **Queen safety**: Black’s queen is on f5, somewhat exposed; however, no immediate tactic wins it.\n- **King safety**: White’s king has moved (KWF1), so castling is not possible, making king safety a concern. Avoid moves that expose the king further.\n\nPromising moves:\n- **Bc1-f4**: Develops the bishop actively, pressures e5 and g5, and indirectly