onsidered unsafe or not legal? But legally, the bishop cannot move vertically — bishops move diagonally only.\n\nAh! Critical mistake.\n\n**Bishops move only diagonally** — not vertically or horizontally.\n\nSo the bishop on f1 cannot move to f2 — that’s vertical, not diagonal.\n\nSimilarly, no pawn is attacking f2. The knight on c3 can’t reach f2. The king on d1 — d1 to f2 is two files right, one rank up — knight move again. Not a king move.\n\nKing can go to c1, c2, d2, e1, e2 — but not f2.\n\nSo **no White piece can currently capture the Black queen on f2**?\n\nBut how did the Black queen get there?\n\nAnd why isn’t it giving check?\n\nUnless... is it a typo?\n\nWait — row 2: f2 is QBF1 — Black Queen, moved, en passant irrelevant.\n\nBut no White piece can capture it immediately.\n\nHowever, Black may lose it next move unless they move it away.\n\nBut from White’s perspective — this is a huge opportunity.\n\nBut none of the valid moves allow capturing on f2.\n\nBut let's double-check: is there any move that ends on f2?\n\nLooking at valid moves:\n\n- P: g2-g3 — no\n- P: h2-h3 — no\n- B: f1-e2 — diagonal, yes\n- But no B: f1-g2? That would be diagonal — f1 to g2 is one up, one right — yes, diagonal.\n\nBut **B: f1-g2** is not in the list.\n\nValid bishop moves: e2, d3, c4, b5, a6 — all long diagonals to the left — meaning the bishop is being developed to the queenside.\n\nBut why not g2 or h3?\n\nPossibly because g2 is occupied? g2 has PWF0 — white pawn — so g2 is occupied by a white pawn. So f1 to g2 would require g2 to be empty — but it's not.\n\nSimilarly, f1 to e2: e2 is empty — yes, so that's valid.\n\nSo bishop can go to e2.\n\nBut no piece can capture the queen on f2.\n\nBut wait — what about the knight on c3?\n\nN: c3-e4 — e4 is empty — yes.\n\nBut none go to f2.\n\nOr king? d1 to e2? Is e2 attacked?\n\ne2 is not under direct attack — but after K: d1-e2, would it be safe?\n\nBut K: d1-e2 is not in the list. Valid king move is only K: d1-c1.\n\nWhy only c1?