ut bishop is on f1.\n\nAfter g2-g3, next move B: f1-g2 could be played, attacking the queen.\n\nBut B: f1-g2 is not currently listed — because g2 will be occupied after g3? No — g2-g3 means pawn leaves g2, goes to g3 — so g2 is empty.\n\nWait — no: g2-g3 means pawn moves from g2 to g3 — so g2 becomes empty.\n\nSo after g2-g3, g2 is empty — so bishop can later go to g2.\n\nBut currently, before the move, g2 is occupied — so B: f1-g2 is not possible now.\n\nBut after g2-g3, then next move B: f1-g2 becomes possible — and attacks f2 (diagonally: g2 to f2 is one left, same rank? No — g2 to f2 is one file left, same rank — horizontal, not diagonal.\n\nBishop moves diagonally — so f1 to g2 is one up, one right — that’s diagonal.\n\nBut g2 to f2 is one left — horizontal — not diagonal.\n\nSo bishop on g2 cannot attack f2.\n\nOnly if it were on g3 — g3 to f2 is one down, one left — diagonal.\n\nYes! So if White plays g2-g3 (pawn to g3), then later B: f1-g2, then B: g2-h3 or something — but to attack f2, bishop needs to be on g3.\n\nBut g3 is occupied by the pawn.\n\nSo pawn on g3 blocks the diagonal.\n\nSo no way to attack f2 with bishop.\n\nOther pieces?\n\nKnight: can knight go to e4 or g4 to threaten f2?\n\nFrom e4, knight attacks f2 — yes: e4 to f2 is one right, two down — knight move.\n\nYes!\n\nSo if White plays **N: c3-e4**, then next move, the knight can go from e4 to f2 — capturing the queen.\n\nBecause f2 is occupied by Black queen — so N: e4-f2 is a capture.\n\nAnd after N: c3-e4, if Black does not move the queen, White plays N: e4-f2 and wins the queen.\n\nBut Black will likely move the queen on their turn.\n\nBut by playing N: c3-e4, White threatens Nxf2 next move.\n\nThis is a powerful threat.\n\nCompare to other moves:\n- P: a2-a4 — no such threat.\n- B: f1-c4 — develops, but allows queen to escape freely.\n\nSo **N: c3-e4** creates an immediate threat to win material.\n\nMoreover, e4 is a strong central square — even if the queen escapes, the knight is well p